<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aliya Rama Raya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aliya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> Raya was a nobleman of the Aravidu family and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> who dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> as regent for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadashiva-raya">Sadashiva Raya</a> from the 1540s. He played the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a> against one another, but his interventions eventually united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> against him. He was captured and beheaded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a> in January 1565.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rama Raya was born in 1484 into a Telugu family. The Aravidu family to which he belonged came from South Andhra, and his mother, Abbaladevi, was the daughter of a chieftain in Nandyala. He and his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirumala-deva-raya">Tirumala Deva Raya</a> both married daughters of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishna Deva Raya</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> tradition he is called Aliya Rama Raya, aliya meaning son-in-law. With another brother, Venkatadri, the Aravidu brothers rose under Krishna Deva Raya, and Rama Raya made his name as a general, administrator and diplomat in the emperor's campaigns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishna Deva Raya</a> died in 1529, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achyuta-deva-raya">Achyuta Deva Raya</a> took the throne, and Rama Raya's influence grew, helped by his alliance with Pemmasani Erra Timmanayudu of the Pemmasani Nayaks. When Achyuta Deva Raya died in 1542 the throne passed to his nephew Sadasiva Raya, then a minor, and Rama Raya became regent. Even after Sadasiva came of age, Rama Raya kept him a virtual prisoner and ruled in his place, replacing many of the kingdom's servants with officers loyal to himself. He also put down revolts by the chieftains of Travancore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragiri">Chandragiri</a>.</p><p>The Deccan Sultanates, divided among themselves, more than once asked him to mediate, and he used their quarrels to push north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> river. He secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur">Raichur</a> doab for Ahmednagar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, fought for Ahmednagar in 1549 and took the fort of Kalyana, and in 1557 joined Ali Adil Shah of Bijapur and the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> against Ahmednagar and Golconda. Some scholars criticise this interference, while P. B. Desai defended it as a way of keeping any one sultanate from rising above the others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His constant changes of side led the sultanates to settle their differences, helped by marriages among their ruling families, and to form what was called a general league of the faithful. In 1565 Rama Raya led the defence against the armies of Husain Nizam Shah, Ali Adil Shah and Ibrahim Qutb Shah at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talikota">Battle of Talikota</a>. He was beheaded, either by Husain Nizam Shah himself or on his orders, although Ali Adil Shah, who had been on friendly terms with him, was against it. The invaders sacked the city of Vijayanagara and massacred its inhabitants, and the empire broke into several semi-independent principalities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rama Raya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive defeat came on 23 January 1565 at the Battle of Talikota, when the combined armies of Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar destroyed the Vijayanagara army and killed its leader <b>Aliya Rama Raya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Talikota, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=16.48,76.31&amp;year=1555" aria-label="Talikota on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6628 1551 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Aliya Rama Raya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7078 1821h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7078 1821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=16.48,76.31&amp;year=1555">What was here in 1555? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
